# travel-facts carries the TEXT layer of the fleet: facts.json, article-list.json,
# state.json, corrections.json, the SQL seeds, the audits and the scripts. That is
# ~33 MB across ~1,700 files and it is the part that is irreplaceable.
#
# The sourced photographs and the rendered books are ~6.8 GB and are NOT carried
# here. They stay on the volume, and this is a STANDING DECISION, not a default
# to be revisited when someone notices they are single-copy:
#
#   GitHub suspends accounts that use repositories as a storage or backup
#   service. That is a term of service, not a size limit, so "it fits" is not
#   the test and Git LFS does not make it allowed. Pushing 6.8 GB of
#   photographs here risks the account that holds the fleet's actual source.
#
# They are also reproducible — re-sourced, or re-rendered from the build sources
# that ARE tracked here — so the thing worth protecting is already protected.
# If the images ever need a second copy, it belongs on another disk, not here.
#
# Note: ignoring a pattern does NOT untrack a file already committed. The PDFs,
# tarballs and sample PNGs uploaded to this repo through the GitHub web UI stay
# tracked and are left alone.

.DS_Store
._*

# sourced photographs and rendered plates
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
*.webp
*.gif
*.tif
*.tiff
*.psd
*.svg.bak

# video
*.mp4
*.mov
*.m4v

# rendered books and archives
*.pdf
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
dossiers-pdf/

# embedded fonts
*.ttf
*.otf
*.woff
*.woff2

# the damaged object store, kept on disk as a backup — not a thing to commit
.git.broken-*/

# python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# every tool here writes a .pre-<label> snapshot before it edits. Hundreds of
# them, several MB each, and all reproducible by re-running the tool.
*.pre-*

# images saved without an extension (the R2 key IS the public_id, hard rule 10),
# so no extension pattern can catch them
**/renamed/

# the single-file book renders: generated output with the photographs inlined as
# base64, 5-15 MB each. The SOURCE that makes them (build.py, the audit gates,
# the chapter files) is tracked; the render is not.
**/dossier/**/_render.html
**/dossier/**/_book.html
**/dossier/**/_sheet.html
**/dossier/**/dossier*.html
